      "id": 1,
      "text": "Toxey began as Thompkinsville, a name honoring John Wesley Pennington, before adopting its current name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/toxey/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Toxey achieved municipal status when it incorporated in 1949, becoming an officially recognized town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/toxey/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Alabama, Tennessee and Northern Railroad reached the area in 1912, sparking rapid growth and connecting Toxey to regional markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/toxey/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Log Cabin School House, built around 1850, is one of the region’s earliest one‑room schools and is listed on the Alabama Register of Landmarks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/toxey_alabama",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Skinner Memorial Park in Toxey preserves the historic Denton and Mattie Mosley log cabin, a tangible link to the town’s early settlers.",
